# -*- mode: org; org-html-head-include-default-style: nil; org-html-postamble: nil; -*-
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil num:nil
#+TITLE: tubo
Tubo is a streaming front-end focused on bringing the [[https://newpipe.net/][NewPipe]] experience to the web. It aims at providing a clean and simple user interface to consume media from various streaming platforms. It currently supports the same services as NewPipe, including YouTube, SoundCloud, Bandcamp, and more.

* Installation
There's a Docker Compose file available which you can run locally like this:

#+begin_src sh
cd /path/to/tubo
docker compose up -d
#+end_src

If you'd rather use the image(s) pushed to DockerHub, you can simply remove the =build= section and add an =image= property for the corresponding service in the =docker-compose.yml= file.

#+begin_src yaml
services:
  frontend:
    image: migalmoreno/tubo-frontend
    # ...
  backend:
    image: migalmoreno/tubo-backend
    # ...
  bg-helper:
    image: migalmoreno/tubo-bg-helper
    # ...

# ...
#+end_src

* Configuration
The main configuration file for all services lies in =resources/config.edn=. You can override this file with your preferred settings. If you use Docker you can use environment variables to configure certain deployment settings like the port where a service is running, or for settings which don't have a corresponding environment variable you can create a bind mount to a custom configuration file in the =backend= service:

#+begin_src yaml
services:
  backend:
    # ...
    volumes:
      - /path/to/your/config.edn:/app/resources/config.edn
#+end_src

* Development Setup
To get a development environment set up, first install the front-end dependencies.

#+begin_src sh
npm i
#+end_src

To start necessary processes alongside the application (database, scripts, etc.), you'll need to invoke the following in the project root:

#+begin_src sh
# Add `-- -D` if you want to detach the processes
nix run .
#+end_src

You can now access the database like this:

#+begin_src sh
psql
#+end_src

Or by accessing a local =pgweb= instance in your browser at [[http://localhost:8081]]

** CLI
To start the front-end development server with hot-reload support, run:

#+begin_src sh
clojure -M:dev:cljs watch frontend
#+end_src

You can now access it in your browser at [[http://localhost:8020]]

To start the API, run:

#+begin_src sh
clojure -M:run
#+end_src

You can now check the API documentation at [[http://localhost:3000/api-docs][http://localhost:3000/api-docs]]

** REPL
You can also work on Tubo using an interactive environment like [[https://docs.cider.mx/cider/index.html][CIDER]]. Go to any Clojure(Script) source file in the project, invoke =M-x cider-jack-in-clj&cljs= and you'll enter a live REPL.

You can then invoke this to start the system:

#+begin_src clojure
user=> (go)
#+end_src
